      "want": "B",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(B) and (H) are the most likely. (B) shoot tips contain meristems, where active cell division occurs. (H) anthers produce pollen via meiosis, but the tissue itself undergoes mitosis during development. (A), (C), (D), (E), (F), (G) are mature tissues with little to no active cell division.\n\nAnswer: (B)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2805",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The light reactions produce ATP and NADPH through the electron transport chain and photophosphorylation, which are the energy and reducing power needed by the Calvin cycle to fix CO2 into sugars. CO2 is taken in from the atmosphere, not produced in the light reactions; oxygen is a byproduct of water splitting, not a reactant in the Calvin cycle.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2806",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Silver fur is the recessive trait (b), so an individual with silver fur must have genotype bb. For two brown-furred parents to produce a bb offspring, both must carry at least one b allele. Option (C) Bb x Bb can produce bb offspring with probability 25%. Option (D) Bb x bb can produce bb with 50%. Option (E) BB x Bb cannot produce bb. Option (F) bb x Bb is the same as (D). Option (H) is invalid (triploid). Option (I) is the same as (D). Option (B) is the same as (E). Option (A) would produce only silver offspring. Option (G) only brown.\n\n(C) is the most general case of two brown-furred parents (heterozygous) producing a recessive phenotype.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2807",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (A).\n\n(A) tRNA is a small non-coding RNA, typically 70–90 nucleotides.\n(B) Substitution doesn't change length.\n(C) mRNA is shorter than the gene due to intron removal, but still hundreds to thousands of nucleotides.\n(D) Pre-mRNA is longer than mRNA (contains introns).\n(E) Duplication increases length.\n(F) Pre-mRNA is longer than mRNA.\n(G) Deletion reduces length, but the segment is still the gene length minus the deletion.\n(H) A DNA segment (gene) is typically hundreds to thousands of nucleotides.\n(I) cDNA is the same length as the mRNA it's made from.\n(J) Point mutation doesn't change length.\n\ntRNA is the only molecule here that is consistently and specifically short (70–90 nt), while all other options involve segments of the gene or its transcripts, which are typically hundreds to thousands of nucleotides.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2808",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(C)\n\nAristotle believed species were fixed and immutable since antiquity. Cuvier, a proponent of catastrophism, also held to the fixity of species, believing new species appeared after catastrophic events. Linnaeus, while classifying species, did not propose any mechanism for their change over time. Lamarck and Darwin both proposed mechanisms for evolution, so they are excluded.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2809",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(A) is incorrect: apoptosis is programmed cell death, not cell division. (B) is correct: apoptosis is conserved across eukaryotes, suggesting an ancient origin. (C) is correct: apoptosis removes webbing between fingers during embryonic development. (D) is correct: apoptosis is a controlled process, unlike necrosis, which releases harmful contents.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2810",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "F",
      "got": "F",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(F)\n\nThe term 'theory' in science means a well-substantiated explanation supported by evidence, not a guess. Evolution by natural selection has been tested and supported by genetics, paleontology, and biogeography. (A) is too strong — it's a theory, not a simple fact. (B) is misleading; it's not 'in the process' of being tested, it's been tested extensively. (C) is false. (D) and (H) use 'hypothesis' or 'guess' incorrectly. (E) and (J) mischaracterize it as opinion or belief. (G) implies it's just one of many plausible explanations, but it's the only one with substantial evidence. (I) is incorrect; it's based on vast data across many species.\n\nAnswer: (F)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2811",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "F",
      "got": "F",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (E).\n\n1. The question asks for the inorganic compound found in living organisms.\n2. (A) oxygen, (B) calcium, (C) carbon, (D) potassium, (E) phosphorus, (G) sulfur, (H) hydrogen, (I) nitrogen are all elements, not compounds.\n3. (F) water (H2O) is a compound, and it is the most abundant inorganic compound in living organisms.\n4. (J) glucose is an organic compound (C6H12O6).\n5. Therefore, water is the correct answer.\n\nAnswer: (F)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2812",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(H) is correct. Homology refers to structures derived from a common ancestor; the more distantly related two species are, the more time has passed since their common ancestor, allowing more mutations and evolutionary changes to accumulate, which typically reduces the number of shared homologous structures compared to more closely related species. (A) mutation rates vary widely across species; (B) DNA sequences diverge over time; (C) phenotype reflects both genetics and environment; (D) chromosome number is not a reliable indicator of relatedness; (E) habitat is unrelated to genetic distance; (F) chromosome structure diverges; (G) is the opposite of the premise; (I) is the opposite of (H); (J) evolution has no single rate.\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
