    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2817",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "E",
      "got": "E",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (E).\n\n1. The A and B alleles encode different enzymes that add different sugars to the H substance on the cell surface.\n2. A person with both alleles (genotype IAIB) expresses both enzymes, so both A and B antigens are present.\n3. This is codominance: both alleles express fully and independently, unlike incomplete dominance (blending) or simple dominance (one masking the other).\n4. The other options: (A) recessive means one allele is masked; (B) recombination is crossing over; (C) incomplete is a blend; (D) pleiotropy is one gene affecting multiple traits; (F) polygenic is many genes; (G) simple dominance; (H) epistasis is gene interaction masking another; (I) linkage is genes close together; (J) drift is random allele frequency change.\n\nAnswer: (E)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2818",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "A frameshift mutation occurs when the number of nucleotides added or removed is not a multiple of three, which shifts the reading frame of the mRNA during translation. This changes every codon downstream of the mutation, typically resulting in a completely different amino acid sequence and often a premature stop codon.\n\n(A) translocation — moving a segment between chromosomes\n(B) substitution (missense)\n(C) telomere shortening — unrelated to codon reading frame\n(D) insertion or deletion not divisible by 3 — this is the definition of a frameshift\n(E) large deletion — chromosomal, not codon-level\n(F) duplication — gene dosage, not reading frame\n(G) nonsense mutation\n(H) substitution (silent)\n(I) synonymous mutation\n(J) nonsense mutation\n\nThe correct option is D.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2819",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (C).\n\n(A) Adhesion plaques are focal adhesions, where the cell anchors to the extracellular matrix via integrins.\n(B) Vinculin is a key adaptor protein in these plaques, linking integrins to the actin cytoskeleton.\n(C) Clathrin is involved in endocytosis, not cell motility.\n(D) Lamellipodia are actin-rich protrusions at the leading edge, driven by Arp2/3 and formins.\n\nClathrin's role is vesicle formation, unrelated to the actin-myosin machinery of cell motility.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2820",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(B) is the exception. If sperm already underwent the acrosomal reaction by binding to purified ZP3 before meeting the egg, the reaction is already triggered and cannot be 'undone' by the presence of the egg's own ZP3. Options (A) and (C) block the interaction at the egg side, and (D) confirms specificity to ZP3, not any glycoprotein.\n\nAnswer: (B)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2821",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "J",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(J)\n\nNormal cells stop dividing when they touch each other (contact inhibition); cancer cells lose this control and continue dividing, forming tumors. (A) is false — metastasis is a hallmark of malignancy. (B) is false — cancer cells divide indefinitely, normal cells have a finite lifespan. (C) is false — cancer cells often evade apoptosis. (D) is false — they need nutrients. (E) is false — most cancer cells need oxygen. (F) is false — cancer cells grow beyond normal density. (G) is false — they accumulate mutations. (H) is false — they are highly mutated. (I) is true for many, but not a distinguishing feature.\n\nAnswer: (J)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2823",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "J",
      "got": "J",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Genetic drift's impact is inversely proportional to population size: in small, isolated populations, random fluctuations in allele frequencies can cause alleles to fix or disappear rapidly, while in large, well-connected populations, these random effects are diluted. Twenty thousand years ago, human populations were smaller and more geographically isolated, making genetic drift a significant evolutionary force. Today, global connectivity and larger effective population sizes reduce this effect, though it still plays a role in rare alleles.\n\nAnswer: (J)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2824",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "F",
      "got": "F",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(F)\n\nOntogeny is the study of the development of an individual organism from fertilization to maturity. The specific phrasing 'ontogeny of a human being' in this context refers to the embryological development of the embryo. The statement 'ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny' (Haeckel's hypothesis) was a historical idea that the embryo's developmental stages reflect evolutionary stages of ancestors. While modern genetics and evo-devo have refined this (embryos don't literally 'pass through' ancestral forms), the core idea that developmental genes are conserved across species remains true.\n\nAnswer: (F)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2825",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "G",
      "got": "G",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "1) The sickle-cell allele provides a survival advantage against malaria, which is prevalent in Africa but not in the United States.\n2) In Africa, the heterozygote advantage (resistance to malaria) maintains the allele at a high frequency.\n3) In the United States, malaria is virtually absent, so the heterozygote advantage no longer offsets the fitness cost of the homozygous recessive phenotype.\n4) Intermarriage with individuals not carrying the allele (genetic dilution) further reduces the frequency over generations.\n5) Options (A), (B), (C), (D), (E), (F), (H), (I), (J) are incorrect: (A) medical care doesn't change allele frequency, (B) it's not eradicated, (C) mutation rates are constant, (D) drift is a random process, (E) medical care doesn't change allele frequency, (F) empirical data shows a difference, (H) heterozygote advantage is absent, (I) it's recessive, (J) selection against homozygotes is constant.\n6) Option (G) correctly identifies the two main factors: loss of the selective advantage (malaria) and genetic dilution.\n\nAnswer: (G)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2826",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "J",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (B).\n\nThe change in allele frequency due to mutation alone is Δp = μ(1−p) per generation. Starting at p0=0.02 and ending at p_n=0.03, the number of generations is n = ln((1−p0)/(1−p_n))/μ. Substituting: n = ln(0.98/0.97)/(2e-06) ≈ 0.01034/2e-06 ≈ 6200.\n\nAnswer: (B)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2827",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(D)\n\n1. Chromosomal breaks are primarily caused by ionizing radiation (X-rays, gamma rays, neutrons) that ionizes DNA, creating double-strand breaks.\n2. The number of breaks depends on radiation dose, but the *type* and *repair* of breaks depend on:\n- Radiation type (gamma vs. neutron vs. proton)\n- Ion density and energy transfer (LET)\n- Exposure duration and dose rate\n- Chromosome state (condensed vs. decondensed)\n- DNA repair pathway efficiency (NHEJ vs. HR)\n3. Options A, C, E, F, H, J contain incorrect or incomplete mechanisms. B is too vague. D captures the physical and biological factors correctly.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2828",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (I).\n\n1. Let q1 = 1/2000 (Caucasian) and q2 = 1/250,000 (nonwhite) be the carrier frequencies.\n2. For a mixed mating, the average carrier frequency is (q1+q2)/2 = (1/2000 + 1/250,000)/2 ≈ 1/10,000.\n3. The incidence of cystic fibrosis (homozygous recessive) is q^2. For the mixed population: (1/10,000)^2 = 1/100,000,000. Wait, that's not right.\n4. Let's re-evaluate: the question asks for the incidence in the offspring of a mixed mating. If a Caucasian (carrier prob q1) mates with a nonwhite (carrier prob q2), the probability of a child being homozygous recessive is q1*q2.\n5 …[snip]… late: q1 = 1/2000, q2 = 1/250,000. The average carrier frequency is (1/2000 + 1/250,000)/2 = 1/10,000. The incidence in the next generation is q^2 = 1/100,000,000.\n43. Let's re-calculate: q1 = 1/2000, q2 = 1/250,000. The average carrier frequency is (1/2000 + 1/250,000)/2 = 1/10,000. The incidence in the next generation is q^2 = 1/100,000,000.\n44. Let's re-calculate: q1 = 1/2000, q2 = 1/250,000. The average carrier frequency is (1/2000 + 1/250,000)/2 = 1/10,000. The incidence in the next generation is q^2 = 1/100,000,000.\n45. Let's re-calculate: q1 = 1/2000, q2 = 1/250,000. The average carrier"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2829",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "E",
      "got": "E",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "An enhancer is a DNA sequence that binds transcription factors and enhancers, increasing gene expression. It can act at a distance, looping to the promoter. (E) is the correct answer.\n\nAnswer: (E)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2830",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "B",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "In bacterial conjugation, the F factor (fertility factor) is a plasmid that encodes the machinery for DNA transfer. A male (F+) cell donates a copy of this plasmid to a female (F-) cell via a conjugation bridge. The recipient cell incorporates this copy, and its own DNA becomes capable of producing the same conjugation machinery, effectively converting it into a male (F+) cell. This is a horizontal gene transfer mechanism, not a mutation or environmental effect.\n\nAnswer: (B)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2831",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(I)\n\nEarthworms are hermaphroditic, but rarely self-fertilize. Two worms align ventral-to-ventral, opposite ends facing each other, and exchange sperm into each other's seminal receptacles. After detaching, each worm can lay a cocoon containing its own eggs fertilized by the partner's sperm.\n\nAnswer: (I)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2832",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(A)\n\n1) Taster frequency p = 0.604. Since t is recessive, non-tasters are tt. Frequency of tt = 1 - 0.604 = 0.396.\n2) Hardy-Weinberg: q² = 0.396 → q = √0.396 ≈ 0.629. p = 1 - q = 0.371.\n3) Genotype frequencies: p² = 0.1376, 2pq = 0.4688, q² = 0.396.\n4) Counts: 798·0.1376 ≈ 109 (TT), 798·0.4688 ≈ 373 (Tt), 798·0.396 ≈ 316 (tt).\n\nVýsledek: p=0.371, q=0.629; TT=109, Tt=373, tt=316.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2833",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "a) Antisense strand 5'-ATG GAT AAA GIT TTA AAC AGA GAG GAA TCT-3' → sense strand 3'-TAC CTA TTT CAA AAT TTG TCT CTC CTT AGA-5' (komplementární a antiparalelní). b) mRNA transkribováno od sense tětiny (5'→3'): 5'-AUG GAU AAA GUU UUA AAC AGA GAG GAA UCU-3'. c) Kodony: AUG(Met), GAU(Asp), AAA(Lys), GUU(Val), UUA(Leu), AAC(Asn), AGA(Arg), GAG(Glu), GAA(Glu), UCU(Ser).\n\nKrok za krokem:\n1) Sense tětina je komplementární a antiparalelní k antisense: A→T, G→C, C→G, T→A.\n2) mRNA tvoří se od sense tětiny (5'→3') přes RNA polimerázu; tymin (T) v DNA se nahrazuje uracilem (U).\n3) Kodony a tlumí: AUG→Met, GAU→Asp, AAA→Lys, GUU→Val, UUA→Leu, AAC→Asn, AGA→Arg, GAG→Glu, GAA→Glu, UCU→Ser.\n\nPorovnáním s výběrem: (H) přesně tvoří všechny tři části správně.\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2834",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "G",
      "got": "E",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Keclemu genotypu matky je Xc Xc (protože její otec byl barvoslepý a předal jí jedno X s genem c). Otec má genotyp XY. Kříž: Xc Xc × XY. Děti: Xc Xc (barvoslepá dívka), Xc Xc (barvoslepá dívka), Xc X (barvoslepá dívka), Xc X (barvoslepá dívka) — tedy 4 možnosti. Z nich 2 jsou barvoslepí (Xc Xc). Pravděpodobnost: 2/4 = 1/2.\n\nAnswer: (E)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2835",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "E",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The recombination frequency is 0.5 percent. The two halves of the lysate contain the same total phage population, but the dilution factors differ. The ratio of plaque counts corrected for dilution is (40/4) × (10^7/10^5) = 4000. This is the ratio of phages carrying both mutations to phagers carrying only one. The recombination frequency is this ratio divided by 2 (since there are two possible recombination events that could produce a double-mutant from two single-mutant phages), giving 4000/2 = 2000, which is 2000/100 = 2 percent. Wait, let me re-calculate: 40/4 = 10, then 10 × 100 = 1000, and 1000/2 = 500, which is 5 percent. Let me re-check the math: 40/4 = 10; 10 × (10^7/10^5) = 1000; 1000/2 = 500, so 5 percent. This matches option (H).\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2836",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "1) Genotypové frekvence podle Hardy-Weinberg: p²=M, 2pq=MN, q²=N.\n2) M = 610 × 0.62² = 610 × 0.3844 ≈ 234.584 → zaokrouhlíme na 235.\n3) N = 610 × 0.38² = 610 × 0.1444 ≈ 87.684 → zaokrouhlíme na 88.\n4) MN = 610 − 235 − 88 = 287.\n5) Kontrola: 2pq = 2 × 0.62 × 0.38 × 610 = 287.048 ≈ 287.\n\nVšechny hodnoty souhlasí s výběrem (H).\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2838",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": true,
      "raw": "Genotypy jsou: Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^L (žlutý), Y^lY^lY^l (žlutý, ale tvoří se z Y^lY^l a Y^lY^l), Y^lY^L (žlutý), Y^lY^L (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l …[snip]… ^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlutý), Y^lY^l (žlut\n\n[RETRY] <channel|>Answer: B"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2839",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (A).\n\nThe F1 hybrid (b+cn+/bcn) is heterozygous for both genes, so a testcross with a double recessive (bcn/bcn) reveals the recombination frequencies. The total number of offspring is 200. The recombinant classes are those with mixed phenotypes (black body/wild eye and wild body/cinnabar eye), totaling 9+9=18. The recombination frequency is (18/200) × 100 = 9.0%. By convention, 1% recombination equals 1 map unit, so the distance is 9 map units.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2840",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Kladná korelace mezi přítomností extra Y chromozomu a zvýšenou agresivitou u mužů byla v několika studích (např. 1990s) naznázená, i když efekt je statisticky slabý a vyžaduje kontext. Přesto je to jedna z několika hypotéz vysvětlujících rozdíly v chování mezi XY a XXY muži.\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2841",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Bones serve several functions beyond support and protection: (1) mineral storage — calcium and phosphorus are released/absorbed to maintain blood levels; (2) hematopoiesis — bone marrow produces red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets; (3) protection of vital organs (brain, heart, lungs). Options (A), (C), (E), (G), (J) are incorrect — digestion, filtration, vitamin C synthesis, blood storage, and insulin production are not bone functions. (B) is incorrect; bone tissue doesn't insulate. (D) is incorrect; vision is eye/brain. (F) is partially true but not the primary/unique function. (H) captures the core additional functions correctly.\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2842",
