      "want": "E",
      "got": "E",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": true,
      "raw": "Prokaryotické buňky (bakterie) chybí t.g. jaderné obal, organely (mitochondrie, ER, Golgi, t.j. t.g. tělka), t.j. t.g. tělka, t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. …[snip]… . t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j. t.j.\n\n[RETRY] Answer: E"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2843",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "A)\n\nBakterie (prokaryoty) nemají tvoříte jádro, tedy jadro obklopené jadernou membránou, ani membránové organely jako Golgi, endoplazmatické síťi nebo mitochondrie. Tělní vlákna (mikrotubuly) tvořící tělísko tvoří se v eukaryota během mitozy/meiózy, což je u bakterií nahrazeno jednoduchým rozdělením genomu. Histony jsou typické pro eukaryotické nukleoproteiny.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2844",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "G",
      "got": "G",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(G) is correct. At the cellular level, distances are typically in the micrometer (10⁻⁶ m) or Angstrom (10⁻¹⁰ m) range, and masses are in the microgram (10⁻⁶ g) or nanogram (10⁻⁹ g) range. The dalton (Da) is the standard unit for molecular mass, where 1 Da = 1.66054 × 10⁻²⁴ g, which is equivalent to 10⁻³ g/1000, making it convenient for expressing molecular masses in the range of 10²–10⁶ Da. Other options use inappropriate units (liters, kilograms, feet) for this scale.\n\nAnswer: (G)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2845",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "B",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": true,
      "raw": "(B)\n\n1. C, H, O, N jsou lehká, hojná a chemicky aktivní prvky.\n2. Tvoří stabilní kovalentní vazby (C-C, C-H, C-O, C-N) a tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří …[snip]… oří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří tvoří t\n\n[RETRY] Answer: B"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2846",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "G",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The trait is transmitted from the mother but never from the father, which is a classic pattern of X-linked inheritance. Since the father passes his Y chromosome to sons and his X chromosome to daughters, a gene located on the X chromosome can be passed from mother to both sons and daughters, while a father can only pass his X chromosome to his daughters (never to his sons, who receive his Y). This pattern is consistent with X-linked inheritance, not any of the other options listed.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2847",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "A)\n\n1. The classical model of a behavioral act (e.g., in ethology or psychology) breaks it down into three stages: the initial motivation/arousal (appetitive phase), the actual execution of the behavior (consummatory act), and the subsequent rest or recovery period.\n2. Option (D) captures this: appetitive behavior (searching/preparing), consummatory act (the action itself), and quiescence (rest after completion).\n3. Other options are related but less precise: (B) is a basic Skinnerian model, (I) is a simple S-R-R chain, (F) is a general motor control description. (D) is the standard ethological breakdown.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2848",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "F",
      "got": "F",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Connective tissue is a diverse group of tissues that provide structural support and hold other tissues together. It's classified into four groups based on their structure and function: bone (hard, mineralized tissue), cartilage (flexible connective tissue), blood (fluid connective tissue), and fibrous connective tissue (dense and loose). Option F correctly captures this classification and function. Option G incorrectly adds adipose tissue as a separate category (it's a type of connective tissue, but not a separate group in the standard four-group classification). Options A, B, C, D, E, H, I, J contain inaccuracies about the scope or function of connective tissue.\n\nAnswer: (F)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2849",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(D) is correct.\n\n1. Courtship is a complex set of behaviors (displays, songs, gifts) that serves multiple functions: identifying a mate of the same species, assessing its fitness, and signaling reproductive readiness.\n2. In many bird species, including the tern, courtship behaviors can trigger hormonal changes in the female that are necessary to initiate egg production.\n3. The fish gift in the tern's case is a specific form of courtship display that communicates the male's ability to forage and provide resources.\n4. Options (A), (B), (E), (G), (I) are too narrow or incorrect. (C) is a possible side effect but not the primary function. (F) and (H) are too simplistic.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2850",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Protozoans are a diverse group of unicellular eukaryotes with significant morphological and physiological differences (ciliates, flagellates, amoeboids), some even showing plant-like features (chloroplasts in Euglenoidea), making a single taxonomic classification problematic. Option (D) correctly captures this diversity and the specific issue of plant-like features.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2851",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Sperm production (spermatogenesis) requires a temperature about 2-3°C lower than core body temperature, which the scrotum provides. Testes located in the abdominal cavity are exposed to core temperature (~37°C), which is too high for normal sperm maturation, leading to infertility. Surgery moves them to the scrotum to restore this temperature gradient.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2852",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The nuclear membrane is a double membrane (two leaflets) that encloses the nucleus, and it contains nuclear pores that regulate the transport of molecules between the nucleus and cytoplasm. Option (C) correctly describes this structure and function. Option (A) is incorrect because the nuclear envelope is continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum. Option (B) is incorrect since it's a double membrane. Option (D) is incorrect; chlorophyll is in chloroplasts. Option (E) is incorrect; it's a double membrane. Option (F) is incorrect; pores are essential. Option (G) is incorrect; it's not rigid. Option (H) is incorrect; transport is regulated. Option (I) is incorrect; communication is active. Option (J) is incorrect; the membrane reforms during cell division.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2853",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The epidermis is the outermost layer of plant tissue, analogous to animal skin in that it serves as the primary protective barrier against environmental factors and pathogens. Palisade and spongy mesophyll are involved in photosynthesis, xylem and phloem in transport, and guard cells regulate gas exchange, but only the epidermis functions as the primary protective outer layer.\n\nAnswer: (I)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2854",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "A",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(A)\n\nCombustion is a rapid, uncontrolled oxidation that releases all energy at once as heat, which would destroy a cell's structure. Aerobic respiration is a controlled, multi-step metabolic pathway (glycolysis, Krebs cycle, electron transport chain) that releases energy in small, manageable packets, capturing it in ATP instead of releasing it all as heat. This gradual release allows the cell to use the energy for work without thermal damage.\n\nAnswer: (A)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2855",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "G",
      "got": "G",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (G).\n\n(A) Size variation doesn't address function. (B) This would suggest a fitness cost, not a lack of function. (C) Location is irrelevant to whether it's vestigial. (D) Comparative anatomy doesn't prove vestigiality. (E) and (I) show it's not essential for survival, but not that it has no function. (F) evolutionary history doesn't prove current function. (G) The presence of lymphoid tissue suggests a role in immune function (housing beneficial bacteria), which contradicts the claim of being purely vestigial. (H) Inflammation is a pathology, not a function. (J) is a claim about digestion, not the overall function.\n\n(G) is the strongest evidence against the vestigial claim.\n\nAnswer: (G)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2856",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Two species becoming less similar over time is the definition of divergent evolution, which occurs when populations of a common ancestor accumulate different adaptations in response to different selective pressures. Convergent evolution is the opposite — unrelated species becoming more similar. Coevolution involves mutual evolutionary change between two species, and adaptive radiation is rapid diversification into many niches.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2857",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The DNA doubles during the S phase of interphase, where DNA replication occurs. G_1 is before replication, G_2 is after, and M phase (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) involves chromosome segregation, not replication.\n\nAnswer: (I)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2858",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(A) Mimetic coloration, (D) Batesian mimicry, (G) warning coloration, (H) cryptic coloration, (I) disruptive coloration, (J) counter shading all describe specific types of coloration, but the general term for blending in with the environment is cryptic coloration. (H) cryptic coloration is the broad category; (I) disruptive coloration breaks up the outline, (J) counter-shading cancels shadows, and (A) mimicry is specific to imitating another organism. The question asks for the general term for blending in.\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2859",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The new variant can interbreed with itself but not with the original phenotype, which is a form of reproductive isolation. In a laboratory setting, this is most likely caused by polyploidy (doubling of chromosomes), which creates an immediate post-zygotic barrier to backcrossing with the original diploid population. Options (A), (E), and (F) describe evolutionary processes over generations, not a sudden reproductive barrier, and (B) and (C) don't explain this specific reproductive isolation. (G) and (H) describe speciation via geographic or ecological separation, but the laboratory context points to a sudden chromosomal event. Polyploidy (I) fits the description perfectly.\n\nAnswer: (I)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2860",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The child's genotype is determined by the combination of alleles from both parents. Type B can be I^B I^B or I^B i, and type AB is always I^A I^B. Type O requires two i alleles. If the B parent is I^B i and the AB parent gives I^B, the child gets I^B I^B (type B); if the AB parent gives i (not possible, AB has no i allele), the child could get I^B i (type B). Since the AB parent has no i allele to pass on, the child must receive at least one I^A or I^B allele from them, so the child cannot have genotype ii (type O). Thus the probability of type O is exactly 0.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2861",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "F",
      "got": "G",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Glycolysis is the first stage of glucose metabolism, occurring in the cytoplasm, and its primary role is to break down glucose into pyruvate while producing a net of 2 ATP and 2 NADH per glucose molecule. The ATP is produced via substrate-level phosphorylation, not oxidative phosphorylation (which occurs later in the mitochondria). Lactic acid production is a fermentation pathway that regenerates NAD+ from NADH, allowing glycolysis to continue anaerobically, but it's not the primary purpose of glycolysis itself. Pyruvate is indeed the precursor for the Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation under aerobic conditions, but the question asks about the general purpose of glycolysis. Option (G) correctly identifies that glycolysis produces ATP via substrate-level phosphorylation, which is its fundamental energy-yielding step.\n\nAnswer: (G)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2862",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "J",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Taiga (taiga) je tvořena borovicemi a je typická pro chladné klima. Tundra má jen trávu a keře, tropický les je teplý, tráva suchá, chaparral třešní, savana trávu s keči, alpská zóna vysokohorská, mokřiny vodní, listnaté lesy opadávají. Taiga odpovídá.\n\nAnswer: (J)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2863",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "H",
      "got": "H",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Glycolysis breaks down glucose into two pyruvate molecules, producing 2 NADH, 2 ATP, and 2 NADH (from NADH dehydrogenase) which are oxidized to NADP+ in the Calvin cycle. The Calvin cycle fixes CO2 using NADPH and ATP, producing G3P (sugar) and regenerating NADP+ and ADP. This matches the product set.\n\nAnswer: (H)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2864",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "Coevolution je koevoluce dvou nebo více druhů, které se vzájemně ovlivňují evolučním tavením. V případě predátor-kořist se predátor vyvíjí na efektivnější lovíky a kořist na lepší únikové mechanismy, což vytváří koevoluce. (A) mikroevoluce je obecný pojem, (B) speciace vznikem nového druhu, (D) adaptivní radiace rozkladem jedné formy na mnoho, (F) konvergentní evoluce podobných znaků u nesourodých skupin, (I) divergentní evoluce opačné. Správná odpověď je (C).\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_biology_2865",
      "category": "biology",
      "want": "I",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(I) Inositol triphosphate (IP3) binds to receptors on the endoplasmic reticulum, opening calcium channels and releasing Ca2+ into the cytoplasm. (A) ATP is a general energy carrier, (B) NO is a gas signaling molecule, (C) GTP is a nucleotide, (D) cGMP is a second messenger but not for calcium release, (E) prostaglandins are lipid mediators, (F) cAMP activates protein kinase A, (G) calmodulin binds calcium but doesn't release it, (H) DAG activates protein kinase C.\n\nAnswer: (I)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_chemistry_3526",
      "category": "chemistry",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "High melting point, poor conductivity, and insolubility in water point to a network covalent structure (like diamond or graphite), where strong covalent bonds extend throughout the entire structure rather than just between discrete molecules. Ionic compounds conduct well in solution, and simple molecular compounds with weak intermolecular forces (van der Waals, hydrogen bonds) have low melting points.\n\n(A) dipole-dipole: intermolecular, weak.\n(B) ionic: conductive in solution, high melting point.\n(C) covalent network: strong covalent bonds throughout, high melting point, poor conductivity, insoluble.\n(D) nonpolar covalent: weak intermolecular forces, low melting point.\n(E) coordinate: specific bonding type, not a general description of a substance's overall bonding.\n(F) London dispersion: weak, all molecules have them.\n(G) van der Waals: general term for weak intermolecular forces.\n(H) metallic: conductive.\n(I) hydrogen: strong intermolecular, but still intermolecular.\n(J) polar covalent: intermolecular.\n\nThe description fits a covalent network structure.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
