    {
      "id": "mmlu_chemistry_3530",
      "category": "chemistry",
      "want": "E",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "1) Write the half-reactions: Mg → Mg2+ + 2e−; Cr3+ + 3e− → Cr.\n2) Balance electrons: 3×(Mg → Mg2+ + 2e−) + (Cr3+ + 3e− → Cr) → 3Mg2+ + Cr.\n3) Add spectator ions: 3Mg(s) + 2Cr(NO3)3(aq) → 3Mg2+(aq) + 2Cr(s) + 6NO3−(aq).\n4) Simplify: 3Mg(s) + 2Cr(NO3)3(aq) → 3Mg2+(aq) + 2Cr(s) + 2NO3−(aq) (after cancelling 4 NO3−).\n5) Option (B) matches this exactly.\n\nAnswer: (B)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_chemistry_3531",
      "category": "chemistry",
      "want": "B",
      "got": "I",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The simplest alkene is ethene (C2H4), which has two carbons connected by a double bond. A double bond consists of one sigma bond and one pi bond, so two carbons connected by a double bond have two sigma bonds between them, plus four C-H sigma bonds, totaling six sigma bonds. Options (B) and (I) are plausible, but (I) is more precise. (A) is false since ethene has no cis-trans isomerism (needs two different groups on each carbon). (C) is false (sigma bonds exist). (D) is true for the carbons, but (I) is a more complete description of the bond count. (E) is false (sp2 hybridization). (F) and (G) are incorrect (only one pi bond). (H) is false.\n\nAnswer: (I)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_chemistry_3532",
      "category": "chemistry",
      "want": "A",
      "got": "F",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The decomposition of hypochlorous acid into chlorine gas and water reduces the concentration of the acid, but the stoichiometry of the reaction with sodium hydroxide (HClO + NaOH → NaCl + H2O) remains unchanged. The equivalence point is determined by the stoichiometry of the reaction, not by the initial concentration or the rate of decomposition, as long as the reaction proceeds to completion. The choice of indicator (like phenolphthalein) depends on the pH range at the equivalence point, which is determined by the acid's strength, not its concentration. A lower concentration of a weak acid shifts the equivalence point pH slightly, but not enough to move the indicator's transition range significantly away from the true equivalence point, unless the acid is extremely dilute. Thus, the endpoint remains very close to the true equivalence point.\n\nAnswer: (F)"
    },
