      "want": "C",
      "got": "B",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The correct answer is (B).\n\nThe Joule-Thomson coefficient is $\\mu = (\\partial T / \\partial p)_s = \\frac{1}{C_p} \\left[ \\left( \\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial p} \\right)_T + \\left( \\frac{\\partial T}{\\partial p} \\right)_V \\right] = \\frac{1}{C_p} \\left[ \\left( \\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial V} \\right)_T + \\frac{1}{V} \\left( \\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial T} \\right)_V \\right]$. Using the thermodynamic relation $(\\partial U / \\partial p)_T = -(\\partial V / \\partial T)_p \\left( \\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial T} \\right)_V = -V \\alpha^2 / C_p$ (where $\\alpha$ is the thermal expansion coefficient) and the ideal gas law …[snip]… o $\\mu = \\frac{1}{C_p} \\left[ \\left( \\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p} \\right)_T + p/T \\right]$.\n\nPlugging in the values:\n- $p = 1.00 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{Pa}$ (1 bar)\n- $T = 50 + 273.15 = 323.15 \\mathrm{K}$\n- $(\\partial H / \\partial p)_T = -3.29 \\times 10^3 \\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{MPa}^{-1} = -3.29 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}$\n- $C_p = 110.0 \\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\n\n$\\mu = \\frac{1}{110.0} \\left[ -3.29 \\times 10^6 + \\frac{1.00 \\times 10^6}{323.15} \\right] = \\frac{1}{110.0} \\left[ -3.29 \\times 10^6 + 3094.4 \\right] \\approx 34.7 \\mathrm{K} \\mathrm{MPa}^{-1}$.\n\nAnswer: (B)"
