    {
      "id": "mmlu_chemistry_3559",
      "category": "chemistry",
      "want": "E",
      "got": "E",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "For an adiabatic process, $\\Delta S = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) + n R \\ln(P_2/P_1)$. Since it's adiabatic, $P_1 V_1^\\gamma = P_2 V_2^\\gamma$, so $P_2/P_1 = (V_1/V_2)^\\gamma = (T_1/T_2)^{\\gamma-1}$. Substituting: $\\Delta S = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) + n R \\ln((T_1/T_2)^{\\gamma-1}) = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) + n R (\\gamma-1) \\ln(T_1/T_2) = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) - n R (\\gamma-1) \\ln(T_2/T_1) = n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) - n C_V \\ln(T_2/T_1) = 0$. This is the definition of an adiabatic reversible process — it's isentropic.\n\nAnswer: (E)"
    },
