      "want": "J",
      "got": "G",
      "ok": false,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The two copper blocks reach a common temperature of 50 °C. The heat lost by the hot block equals the heat gained by the cold one: m1·c·(T1−T_avg) = m2·c·(T_avg−T2). Since m1=m2 and c is constant, this gives T_avg = (100+0)/2 = 50 °C. The heat transferred is Q = m·c·ΔT = 10000·0.385·50 = 192500 J. The heat lost by the hot block is positive, so the change in enthalpy of the hot block is ΔH = -Q = -192500 J. Dividing by 1000 to get the answer in kJ: ΔH = -192.5 kJ.\n\nAnswer: (G)"
