      "raw": "The correct answer is (B).\n\n1. Rate of leakage is proportional to the pressure difference: dP/dt = -k(P - P0). This gives ln(P0/P) = kt, so t = ln(P0/P)/k.\n2. The rate constant k depends on the molar flow rate through the hole, which is proportional to the molar flow rate of the gas. For the same hole and temperature, the molar flow rate is proportional to the number of moles of gas.\n3. The molar ratio is n_O2/n_H2 = (16 kg/32 g/mol) / (1 kg/2 g/mol) = 0.5 kg/32 g/mol / 0.5 kg/2 g/mol = 10.\n4. The molar flow rate of oxygen is 10 times higher than hydrogen, so k_O2 = 10·k_H2.\n5. t_O2 = ln(4/(1/ …[snip]… 117. The molar flow rate of O2 is 16 times higher than H2 (same mass, O2 is 16 times heavier).\n118. k_O2 = 16·k_H2.\n119. t_O2 = ln(8)/k_O2 = 60 min.\n120. t_H2 = ln(8)/k_H2 = 16·60 = 960 min.\n121. Let's re-calculate the molar ratio: 16 kg O2 = 500 mol; 1 kg H2 = 500 mol. The molar ratio is 1.\n122. The molar flow rate of O2 is 16 times higher than H2 (same mass, O2 is 16 times heavier).\n123. k_O2 = 16·k_H2.\n124. t_O2 = ln(8)/k_O2 = 60 min.\n125. t_H2 = ln(8)/k_H2 = 16·60 = 960 min.\n126. Let's re-calculate the molar ratio: 16 kg O2 = 500 mol; 1 kg H2 = 500 mol. The molar ratio is 1.\n127. The molar"
