    {
      "id": "mmlu_math_7738",
      "category": "math",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "The degree of the composition of two polynomials is the product of their degrees. Let deg(f)=6, deg(g)=3, and deg(h)=n. The degree of f(g(x)) is 6·3=18, the degree of g(h(x)) is 3·n, and the degree of h(f(x)) is n·6. The sum of three polynomials has degree equal to the maximum of their individual degrees (unless the leading terms cancel, which is not the case here). We're told the total degree is 36, so max(18, 3n, 6n) = 36. Since 18 < 36, it must be that 3n=36 or 6n=36. If 3n=36, n=12; if 6n=36, n=6. However, if n=6, then 3n=18, so max(18, 18, 36) = 36, which works. If n=12, then 6n=72, which would give degree 72, not 36. Thus n must be 6.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mmlu_math_7739",
      "category": "math",
      "want": "C",
      "got": "C",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(C) is the correct answer.\n\n(A) No: f′(c) ≠ 0 means the function is strictly increasing or decreasing at c, so c cannot be a local extremum.\n(B) No: f′(c) = 0 is a necessary but not sufficient condition for an extremum (e.g., f(x) = x³ at x=0).\n(C) Yes: differentiability at c implies continuity at c by the definition of the derivative.\n(D) No: differentiability requires continuity.\n(E) No: the statement is a contradiction; if f is differentiable at c, f′(c) must exist by definition.\n(F) No: continuity doesn't imply an extremum.\n(G) No: f(x) = |x| is continuous at 0 but not differentiable.\n(H) No: f′′(c) = 0 is necessary but not sufficient for an inflection point (e.g., f(x) = x⁴ at x=0).\n(I) No: f′(c) = 0 only at one point doesn't make the function constant.\n(J) No: f′(c) ≠ 0 is unrelated to inflection points.\n\nAnswer: (C)"
    },
