      "category": "physics",
      "want": "D",
      "got": "D",
      "ok": true,
      "retried": false,
      "raw": "(D) Asteroids move relative to the background stars. This apparent motion is the primary way they are detected in surveys — they shift position over time, unlike distant stars.\n\n(A) is wrong: asteroids are faint, not bright. (B) rotation doesn't affect visibility. (C) size doesn't matter for detection. (E) color is not a reliable indicator. (F), (G), (H), (I) are physically incorrect.\n\nAnswer: (D)"
    },
